; This is 901483-03.bin - from an 8050 with Micropolis disk drive mechanisms 
;
; Comments Copyright (C) 2025 Piers Finlayson <piers@piers.rocks>
;
; Comments were created with assistance from AI, so take with a pinch of salt!
;
; This file is the 1KB secondary processor's firmware from the 6530 IC.  It
; differs substantially in its operation from the 2040/3040/4040 secondary
; processor firmware, both DOS 1 and DOS 2 versions (901466-02/04).
;
; The 2040/3040/4040 secondary processor firmware is mostly self contained - it
; boots, in the case of the DOS 2 version does a zero page test, initializes
; zero page and shared RAM, and then runs a job checking loop, waiting for
; commands from the primary processor.
;
; This firmware, on the other hand, does very, very little off its own bat.
; It sets one byte of shared RAM ($0400) to 2, waits for it to be set to 0,
; sets it to 2 again, waits for it to be set to 0 again, and then jumps to,
; which is a shared RAM address that the primary processor has copied code to.
; That code does a bunch of self testing and initialization, and provides the
; main operating loop for the secondary processor.  This firmware primarily
; contains
; - a bunch of utilities used by code dynamically loaded by the primary
; - the interrupt handler routine, which runs on a timer (triggered by the
;   6530), which does stuff like ensure the stepper motor steps to the desired
;   track.
;
; This change was presumably instigated to make it much easier to update the
; disk drive - by having less critical code in ths 6530 (mask programmed) ROM,
; but instead having it in the (more easily sourced) primary processor's ROMs.
; It probably helped that the 8050/8250 primary processor ROMs had a total of
; 16KB of space, vs 12KB on the 2040/3040/4040.
;


; da65 V2.19 - Git 162bc6b30
; Created:    2025-05-18 16:34:03
; Input file: ./901483-03.bin
; Page:       1

        .setcpu "6502"

L0017           := $0017
L0500           := $0500

; Once the secondary processor's initialization routine has completed (which
; comes from the primary processor), it does an indirect JMP ($FC00) - which
; causes it to jump to the code at $FC14 given the below.
LFC00:
        .byte   $14
        .byte   $FC

; This points to another routine - LFEA1.  From the primary processor's code
; this appears to be on an error - so presumably FEA1 is an error handler.e
LFC02:
        .byte   $A1
        .byte   $FE

; Reset vector jumps to here
;
; This code sets $0400 ($1000 for the primary processor) to 2, waits for the
; primary processor to set it to 0, then sets $0400 to 1 and waits for the
; primary processor to set it to 0 again.  It then jumps to $500, which the
; primary processor has presumably loaded with some code to execute.
;
; The primary processor at this point appeaars to load "formatcode_2070" to
; the job site, which beings LDX #$3F, TXS, CLD, which is settings the stack
; and clearing the decimal mode.  So, it appears, the primary processor is
; providing code which this processor will run to initialize itself.
;
; It then appears to set the entire zero-page/stack with $3F.  It then appears
; to test address at $3F by incrementing it by 1 256 times and check it's still
; $3F.  Basically it appears to be running a zero page memory test.
;
; I suspect the primary processor has the secondary processor's initialization
; code because it's only required once - whereas this ROM (being on 1KB)
; stores the bulk of the code that is actually needed during run-time. 
LFC04:
        ldy     #$02
LFC06:  sty     $0400   ; Store 02 at the start of shared RAM
LFC09:  lda     $0400   ; Read the value back into A
        bne     LFC09   ; Wait in loop until primary CPU sets $0400 to 0
        bne     LFC06   ; This will never be taken (A is already 0 to get here)
        jmp     L0500   ; Jump to the code copied to RAM by primary CPU

